.TH YES 1
.SH NAME
yes \- print a string repeatedly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yes
[
string
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Yes
writes
.I string
followed by a newline to the standard output, over and over, until it is stopped.  With no
argument the string is
.BR y .
.PP
It is a filter's answer to a program that asks questions: piping
.I yes
into a command supplies the same reply to each of them.
.PP
.B Note:
.B this page is new.
.I Yes
is one of the two commands in the v7
.B /usr/src/cmd
tree that came with no manual page anywhere in the system; the other is dmesg.
The program is eight lines and v7 evidently did not think it needed one.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
None.
.SH BUGS
.I Yes
has no terminating condition of its own.
What normally stops it is the reader at the far end of its pipe going away, which costs it
a SIGPIPE; a
.I yes
whose output is a file or a terminal runs until it is killed.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kill(1), pipe(2), signal(2)
